{
  "format": "srwb",
  "format_version": "1.0",
  "exported_at": "2026-03-12T00:00:00.000Z",
  "notebook": {
    "id": "nb-prolog-gen-r-001",
    "name": "Prolog Generates R: Compiler Demo",
    "description": "Demonstrates UnifyWeaver compiling a Prolog factorial predicate into R code, using the linear recursion compiler and Notebook VFS.",
    "kernelLanguage": null,
    "cellCounter": 13,
    "cells": [
      {
        "code": "# Prolog Generates R — UnifyWeaver Compiler Demo\n\nThis workbook demonstrates **UnifyWeaver** compiling Prolog logic into executable R code — live, inside a notebook.\n\nThe workflow:\n1. Load the UnifyWeaver compiler environment\n2. Define a recursive `factorial/2` in Prolog\n3. The compiler reads the Prolog source from the previous cell via `nb_read/3`, consults it, and generates an equivalent R `for` loop\n4. The generated R code is written to another cell via `nb_write/3`\n5. Run the R cell to verify correctness",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 1: Load UnifyWeaver\n\nLoad the UnifyWeaver environment and the R target compiler.\n\n**Note:** In swipl-wasm, use `use_module(unifyweaver(...))` (search path alias) instead of `use_module(library(...))`. The `['../init'].` call sets up the `unifyweaver` search path so modules resolve correctly.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "['../init'].\n:- use_module(unifyweaver(targets/r_target)).\n:- use_module(unifyweaver(core/advanced/linear_recursion)).",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "load_uw",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 2: Define Factorial in Prolog\n\nA simple recursive factorial. The base case returns 1 when N is 0. The recursive case computes `N * factorial(N-1)`.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": ":- dynamic factorial/2.\n\nfactorial(0, 1).\nfactorial(N, F) :-\n    N > 0,\n    N1 is N - 1,\n    factorial(N1, F1),\n    F is N * F1.",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "prolog_factorial",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 3: Compile Prolog → R\n\nThe compiler:\n1. Reads the Prolog source from the **prolog_factorial** cell via `nb_read/3`\n2. Consults it to assert the clauses into the database\n3. Calls `compile_linear_recursion/3` which detects the recursion pattern, extracts the step (`N1 is N - 1` → step=1, down), and generates an R `for` loop\n4. Writes the generated R code to the **r_factorial** cell via `nb_write/3`\n\nThe `memo(false)` option requests loop-based compilation instead of memoized reduce.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "%% Read the Prolog source from the previous cell\nuser:nb_read('prolog_factorial', '.code', PrologSrc),\nformat('Read from prolog_factorial:~n~w~n~n', [PrologSrc]),\n\n%% Consult it to assert the clauses\nsetup_call_cleanup(\n    open('/tmp/_nb_src.pl', write, Out),\n    write(Out, PrologSrc),\n    close(Out)),\nconsult('/tmp/_nb_src.pl'),\n\n%% Compile factorial/2 → R\nr_target:init_r_target,\nlinear_recursion:compile_linear_recursion(factorial/2, [target(r), memo(false)], Code),\nformat('--- Generated R code ---~n~w~n', [Code]),\n\n%% Write compiler output to the R cell\nuser:nb_write('r_factorial', '.code', Code),\nwriteln('\\nR code written to cell: r_factorial').",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "compile_to_r",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 4: Run the Generated R Code\n\nThe cell below will contain the compiler-generated `factorial` function. Run it to define the function, then run Step 5 to test it.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "# This cell will be overwritten by the compiler.\n# Run the 'compile_to_r' cell above first.",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "r_factorial",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 5: Test the Generated R Code\n\nCall the compiler-generated `factorial` function to verify it produces correct results.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "cat(\"6! =\", factorial(6), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"20! =\", factorial(20), \"\\n\")\ncat(\"0! =\", factorial(0), \"\\n\")",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "test_r",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "## Step 6: Inspect via Bash\n\nThe Notebook VFS lets any kernel inspect cells. Here Bash reads the compiler-generated code:",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      },
      {
        "code": "echo \"=== Cells ===\"\nls /nb/\necho \"\"\necho \"=== r_factorial code ===\"\ncat /nb/r_factorial/.code\necho \"\"\necho \"=== r_factorial language ===\"\ncat /nb/r_factorial/.language",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "bash",
        "name": "inspect",
        "lastOutput": "",
        "lastOutputHtml": ""
      }
    ]
  }
}
